# 8. PAGE & FEATURE PERMISSION MANAGEMENT

The School Owner must control role assignments and page access.

This is a core functional requirement.

However, you must inspect the existing authorization architecture before selecting an implementation.

Do not automatically create a new RBAC framework.

## 8.1 Role assignment

`APP_ADMIN` must be able to:

- View users and their assigned roles.
- Assign approved roles through an authorized management workflow.
- Update authorized role assignments.
- Appoint or replace a Manager.
- Manage the applicable school role configuration.

Do not invent unrestricted multi-role membership if the existing model supports only one primary role.

First identify whether the current system supports single-role or multi-role assignments.

If the requirements cannot be met without changing this model, describe the necessary decision and STOP before mutation.

## 8.2 Page access

`APP_ADMIN` must be able to configure which roles may access additional school pages and features.

The implementation must distinguish at least these concepts:

- Page visibility
- Page entry permission
- Feature/action permission
- Resource-level data authorization

Do not assume these are already separate technical constructs.

Inspect current ownership and implement only what is required and approved.

## 8.3 Example

A Teacher may have access to a class page.

Administrative Personnel may also access that page if authorized.

A Manager may access the corresponding management page.

This does not mean every one of them has identical write permissions or unrestricted access to all student records.

The backend must enforce the applicable authorization rules.

## 8.4 Shared employee features

Employees may share some or many pages without being assigned identical roles.

Examples:

- Teacher and Administrative Personnel may share some student-document functions.
- Administrative Personnel and Manager may share registration management.
- School Staff and relevant administrative employees may share food or operational pages.
- APP_ADMIN and MANAGER may share management dashboards and school administration.

Reuse existing common functionality rather than duplicating it.

## 8.5 Prevent privilege escalation

Ordinary employees must not be able to grant themselves new permissions.

Users must not be able to edit their own role through a profile form unless explicitly authorized.

Role assignment APIs must be protected.

Page authorization must not rely exclusively on frontend checks.

## 8.6 Permission changes

Permission changes must take effect through the application's established authorization/session mechanism.

Inspect how sessions, caches, and role claims are currently handled.

Ensure the design does not leave revoked permissions valid indefinitely.

Do not invent a session architecture to satisfy this requirement.

If current behavior is unclear, record it as UNKNOWN.

